#speculation(title: [Thalamic Lac/tCr as a Candidate Stratification Biomarker for Energy-Metabolism Trials])[
*(Certainty: 0.40. Raw 0.40, population 1.00, discounted 0.40.)* The Vienna experimental-hypoxia probe reported elevated resting thalamic lactate (Lac/tCr ~27% higher in ME/CFS than controls) alongside blunted metabolic reactivity to hypoxia @Bader2026Hypoxia.
Elevated baseline brain lactate could therefore serve as a mechanism-based stratification biomarker for trials of tissue-oxygenation or mitochondrial interventions (e.g., hyperbaric oxygen therapy), identifying a biologically coherent "virtual-hypoxia" subgroup hypothesized to respond @Hadanny2024HBOTLongCovid.

*(Origin: brainstorm.)*
*Replication status:* Not independently replicated; single-site preprint.
*Severity applicability: Unknown — cohort not stratified by severity.*
*Limitations:* Thalamic Lac/tCr is a research MRS measurement, not a validated clinical assay; reproducibility and discrimination (AUC versus healthy controls) are untested. It is _not_ a validated diagnostic — hypothesis-generating only.

*Falsifiable prediction:* Thalamic Lac/tCr will (a) separate ME/CFS from controls with AUC $≥$0.70 in a replication cohort, (b) be reproducible across test–retest, and (c) predict differential response to an energy-targeted intervention in a stratified trial — refuted if it fails any of these.

*Consequence:* A brain-scan lactate measure could help identify a biologically defined "brain-energy deficient" ME/CFS subgroup and match those patients to energy-targeting treatments, providing an objective change endpoint instead of symptom questionnaires — but only after prospective validation.
] <spec:thalamic-lactate-stratification>
